{% extends "layout.twig" %}

{% block title %}Sources — Voyager Distance Tracker{% endblock %}

{% block content %}

<div class="page-header">
  <div>
    <h6 style="color:var(--color-accent)">SOURCES</h6>
    <h1>Where this data comes from</h1>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="detail-two-col">

  <div class="card elev-sm" style="padding:var(--space-4)">
    <span class="card-kicker">Live data</span>
    <span class="card-title"><i class="ph ph-compass"></i> JPL Horizons</span>
    <p class="card-body">Distance from the Sun, speed, and one-way light time all come from NASA JPL's Horizons System — the same ephemeris service that powers most professional solar-system navigation software. No authentication required. Each probe needs two separate requests, since Horizons only accepts one observing center per call: Sun-centered for distance and speed, Earth-centered for distance and light time.</p>
    <a href="https://ssd.jpl.nasa.gov/api/horizons.api" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="btn btn-ghost">ssd.jpl.nasa.gov/api/horizons.api <i class="ph ph-arrow-up-right"></i></a>
  </div>

  <div class="card elev-sm" style="padding:var(--space-4)">
    <span class="card-kicker">Live data</span>
    <span class="card-title"><i class="ph ph-broadcast"></i> DSN Now</span>
    <p class="card-body">Signal/contact status comes from NASA's official Deep Space Network feed — the same XML data that drives the eyes.nasa.gov DSN visualization. A probe frequently shows "not in contact"; that's real dish-scheduling behavior, not an error state.</p>
    <a href="https://eyes.nasa.gov/dsn/data/dsn.xml" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="btn btn-ghost">eyes.nasa.gov/dsn/data/dsn.xml <i class="ph ph-arrow-up-right"></i></a>
  </div>

</div>

<div class="card elev-sm" style="padding:var(--space-4);margin-bottom:var(--space-6)">
  <span class="card-kicker">Periodically updated</span>
  <span class="card-title"><i class="ph ph-heartbeat"></i> Instrument status</span>
  <p class="card-body">There's no public API that decodes actual Voyager telemetry to instrument-level status, so each probe's Instrument Health table is transcribed by hand from NASA's own status page rather than fetched live. Updated here whenever NASA's page changes.</p>
  <a href="https://science.nasa.gov/mission/voyager/where-are-voyager-1-and-voyager-2-now/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="btn btn-ghost">science.nasa.gov &mdash; Where are Voyager 1 and 2 now? <i class="ph ph-arrow-up-right"></i></a>
</div>

<div class="card elev-sm" style="padding:var(--space-4);margin-bottom:var(--space-6)">
  <span class="card-kicker">Periodically updated</span>
  <span class="card-title"><i class="ph ph-clock-counter-clockwise"></i> Mission milestones</span>
  <p class="card-body">The Milestones timeline is hand-compiled, not fetched live. Launch dates, planetary flybys, and heliopause crossings are well-established public mission history; the more recent entries (2023 onward — comms anomalies, thruster events, and the power-conserving instrument shutdowns) are each verified against a specific NASA or JPL announcement:</p>
  <ul class="text-muted" style="margin:0;padding-left:1.1em;font-size:12px;line-height:1.9">
    <li><a href="https://www.nasa.gov/news-release/nasas-voyager-2-probe-enters-interstellar-space/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NASA — Voyager 2 enters interstellar space</a></li>
    <li><a href="https://www.jpl.nasa.gov/news/nasas-voyager-1-resumes-sending-engineering-updates-to-earth/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">JPL — Voyager 1 resumes sending engineering updates</a></li>
    <li><a href="https://www.jpl.nasa.gov/news/voyager-1-returning-science-data-from-all-four-instruments/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">JPL — Voyager 1 returns science data from all four instruments</a></li>
    <li><a href="https://www.jpl.nasa.gov/news/voyager-1-team-accomplishes-tricky-thruster-swap/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">JPL — Voyager 1 team accomplishes tricky thruster swap</a></li>
    <li><a href="https://www.jpl.nasa.gov/news/nasa-turns-off-science-instrument-to-save-voyager-2-power/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">JPL — NASA turns off science instrument to save Voyager 2 power</a></li>
    <li><a href="https://www.jpl.nasa.gov/news/nasa-turns-off-two-voyager-science-instruments-to-extend-mission/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">JPL — NASA turns off two Voyager science instruments to extend mission</a></li>
    <li><a href="https://www.jpl.nasa.gov/news/nasas-voyager-1-revives-backup-thrusters-before-command-pause/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">JPL — Voyager 1 revives backup thrusters before command pause</a></li>
    <li><a href="https://science.nasa.gov/blogs/voyager/2026/04/17/nasa-shuts-off-instrument-on-voyager-1-to-keep-spacecraft-operating/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NASA Science — Voyager 1's last charged-particle instrument shut off</a></li>
    <li><a href="https://science.nasa.gov/blogs/voyager/2026/08/04/nasa-engineers-help-prolong-voyager-2s-science-mission/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NASA Science — Power swap prolongs Voyager 2's science mission</a></li>
  </ul>
</div>

<div class="card elev-sm" style="padding:var(--space-4);margin-bottom:var(--space-6)">
  <span class="card-kicker">Static asset</span>
  <span class="card-title"><i class="ph ph-image"></i> About page background</span>
  <p class="card-body">The Voyager illustration behind the About page text is a NASA render, with its starfield background removed by hand — not fetched live.</p>
  <a href="https://science.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2017/12/voyager.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="btn btn-ghost">science.nasa.gov &mdash; Voyager illustration <i class="ph ph-arrow-up-right"></i></a>
</div>

<div class="card elev-sm" style="padding:var(--space-4);margin-bottom:var(--space-6)">
  <span class="card-kicker">Static data</span>
  <p class="card-body">Launch date, heliopause crossing date, and constellation heading are real facts, but baked into the app rather than fetched live — they don't change often enough to need a live source.</p>
</div>

<p class="text-muted" style="font-size:12px">Live figures are cached locally and refresh at most once every 15 minutes. If a refresh fails, the last known good values are shown rather than breaking the page.</p>

{% endblock %}
